WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.338
A .

2
00:00:01.338 --> 00:00:15.992
NET application may handle JSON, XML, binary formats, form data, configuration files, queue messages, event messages, cache entries, cookies, session values, import files, and API responses in the same overall workflow.

3
00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:23.486
Each format has different tooling, operational expectations, compatibility concerns, and security review points.

4
00:00:23.486 --> 00:00:27.166
The important question is not only what format is used.

5
00:00:27.166 --> 00:00:33.790
It is where the data came from, who controls it, and what the application will do after reading it.

6
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:44.630
Trust boundaries exist between users, browsers, APIs, services, background jobs, databases, caches, queues, file systems, administrators, and third-party systems.

7
00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.578
A value can cross more than one boundary over its lifetime.

8
00:00:48.578 --> 00:00:58.748
An ExampleEventMessage might be created by a service, stored in a queue, retried later by a worker, archived for audit, and then read by a support tool.

9
00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:03.967
Each step can change who can influence the data and what assumptions are safe.

10
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:07.179
Internal data is not automatically trusted data.

11
00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:10.592
A cache entry may have been produced by older code.

12
00:01:10.592 --> 00:01:13.536
A database row may contain imported content.

13
00:01:13.536 --> 00:01:18.621
A queue message may be replayed after a deployment changed the object model.

14
00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:23.305
A configuration file may be edited by a different operational process.

15
00:01:23.305 --> 00:01:36.018
If serialized data affects business decisions, authorization decisions, state changes, or sensitive output, it should be validated for that use, even when it is read from an internal system.

16
00:01:36.018 --> 00:01:38.360
Format choice should be deliberate.

17
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:45.118
JSON is often suitable for web APIs and service messages because it is widely supported and readable.

18
00:01:45.118 --> 00:01:50.337
XML may be needed for established integrations or document-oriented workflows.

19
00:01:50.337 --> 00:01:59.236
Binary formats may be appropriate in controlled internal systems, but they often require stronger ownership and migration discipline.

20
00:01:59.236 --> 00:02:05.392
Custom formats should be rare because they increase maintenance cost and make review harder.

21
00:02:05.392 --> 00:02:07.868
Contracts help teams avoid ambiguity.

22
00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:20.514
A message contract should define required fields, optional fields, ownership, expected producer, expected consumer, versioning rules, and whether the data may be replayed or imported later.

23
00:02:20.514 --> 00:02:26.737
Without that documentation, teams end up relying on tribal knowledge and serializer defaults.

24
00:02:26.737 --> 00:02:32.358
That makes code review harder and turns simple changes into compatibility surprises.

25
00:02:32.358 --> 00:02:37.309
Review who can create, modify, replay, import, or consume serialized data.

26
00:02:37.309 --> 00:02:44.669
A user-created ExampleImportFile should receive different treatment from a server-created ExampleSessionValue.

27
00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:51.695
A partner-supplied document should not inherit the trust level of the internal workflow that receives it.

28
00:02:51.695 --> 00:02:56.914
Secure serialization depends on clear ownership and realistic trust decisions.
